findings.body:
The `lazy(() => import(...))` calls are at module scope and are not wrapped in an `IS_DEV` check. The `IS_DEV` gate only lives in the component body. The bundler still sees the static `import('./scan')` and `import('./devtools')`, so it emits separate chunks for `react-scan` and `@tanstack/react-devtools` in production. Those chunks are never requested at runtime, so there is no runtime cost, but the PR description says the libraries are 'only loaded in development mode' and 'not included in the main production bundle'. Both claims are true for the main bundle and runtime loading, but the code still ships as inert build artifacts.

::diff{file="web/app/components/devtools/react-scan/loader.tsx" lines="6-13"}

::callout{type="info"}
The base code had the same module-scope `lazy()` pattern, so this is not a regression. The PR just does not get the chunk-elimination benefit it describes.

If you want production builds to skip emitting these chunks entirely, put the `lazy()` behind the same compile-time check so the bundler can drop the `import()`:

::suggestion{file="web/app/components/devtools/react-scan/loader.tsx" lines="6-13"}
const ReactScan = IS_DEV
  ? lazy(() => import('./scan').then(module => ({ default: module.ReactScan })).catch((error) => {
      console.error('Failed to load React Scan devtools:', error)
      return { default: () => null }
    }))
  : () => null

When a chunk import fails, the React Scan loader logs `console.error('Failed to load React Scan devtools:', error)`, while the TanStack loader silently returns a null component with no logging. The two loaders are meant to follow the same pattern, but a failed TanStack devtools import will be invisible to a developer debugging why the panel never appears.

::diff{file="web/app/components/devtools/tanstack/loader.tsx" lines="6-11"}

Pick one convention for both loaders. Logging in both is the more useful option, since these are dev-only tools and a silent failure hides the cause.
